### STEP 1 -- a 3000-range list applied to a port denies the matching flow
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 3000 deny icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#access-group 3000
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list 3000
      4 deny icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.48
  interface port1.0.48
   switchport
   switchport mode access
   switchport access vlan 100
   access-group 3000
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 100% packet loss      DENIED
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.66 ->   0% packet loss      PERMITTED
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters
  Name: 3000
      Rule: deny icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
      Packet Hits: 4                        <-- exactly the 4 denied pings
  => STEP 1 PASS: a numbered hardware IP ACL bound to a port denies the traffic its rule
     matches and leaves an identical flow from another source untouched, with the hit counter
     corroborating the packet count.

### STEP 2 -- D4, non-IP traffic unaffected
host# ip neigh flush dev eth4 ; ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 (denied)
host# ip neigh show dev eth4
  10.38.22.70 lladdr 00:00:cd:42:30:29 REACHABLE
  => STEP 2 PASS: ARP re-resolved across the path while ICMP from the same host was denied.
     An IP hardware ACL does not filter non-IP frames.

### STEP 3 -- FINDING: a bound numbered ACL CANNOT BE EDITED
The case's step text ("Configure hardware IP 3000 range ACL for each of the parameters") does
not anticipate this, and it shapes how the rest of the case had to be run.
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 3000 deny ip 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
  % This access-list is currently attached to an interface so cannot be edited.
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list 3000
      4 deny icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32    <-- unchanged; the entry was NOT added
Also measured: the bare `access-list 3000` with no action is rejected with
  % Incomplete command.
  => STEP 3 done: a numbered hardware ACL is read-only while an `access-group` binds it. It
     must be unbound before it can be edited, or a separate list used per rule. Note this is
     the OPPOSITE of the named hardware ACL sub-mode, where re-entering a bound list prints
     `% This access-list is attached to an interface...` as an ADVISORY and the edit DOES
     apply on `end`. The two messages differ by one word and mean opposite things.

### STEP 4 -- all five protocol forms, one list each (per the case's "attached each
###           access-list to different ports"), and their SELECTIVITY
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.48 ; no access-group 3000
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 3001 deny ip    10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 3002 deny proto 1 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 3003 deny tcp   10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 3004 deny udp   10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ#show access-list
  Hardware IP access list 3000     4 deny icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
  Hardware IP access list 3001     4 deny ip   10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
  Hardware IP access list 3002     4 deny icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32   <-- see note
  Hardware IP access list 3003     4 deny tcp  10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
  Hardware IP access list 3004     4 deny udp  10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
  NOTE: `deny proto 1` was CANONICALISED to `deny icmp` in list 3002. Protocol 1 is ICMP, so
  the CLI stores the named form. Worth knowing when diffing a config against what was typed.

Each list bound to port1.0.48 in turn, ICMP offered from both sources:

  list  stored rule   .65 (ICMP)          .66 (ICMP)       expected
  3000  deny icmp     100% packet loss    0% packet loss   DENY  (icmp matches)
  3001  deny ip       100% packet loss    0% packet loss   DENY  (ip matches all IP)
  3002  deny icmp     100% packet loss    0% packet loss   DENY  (proto 1 = icmp)
  3003  deny tcp        0% packet loss    0% packet loss   PASS  (tcp does not match icmp)
  3004  deny udp        0% packet loss    0% packet loss   PASS  (udp does not match icmp)

  => STEP 4 PASS: all five protocol forms are accepted, bind to a port and behave correctly.
     The last two rows are the important ones: a TCP rule and a UDP rule both leave ICMP
     alone, so the protocol field genuinely selects rather than every rule matching
     everything. The three DENY rows and the two PASS rows are each other's controls.

### STEP 5 -- the PERMIT inverse, proven by its counter
Hardware ACLs implicitly PERMIT what no rule denies, so "traffic passed" is NOT proof that a
permit rule worked. The counter is.
x960-56YSQ(config)#access-list 3005 permit icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.48 ; access-group 3005
host# ping .70 from 10.38.22.65 -> 0% packet loss
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters 3005
      Rule: permit icmp 10.38.22.65/32 10.38.22.70/32
      Packet Hits: 3
host# ping -c 4 .70 from 10.38.22.65
x960-56YSQ#show access-list counters 3005
      Packet Hits: 7                        <-- +4 for 4 pings
  => STEP 5 PASS: the permit rule's counter advances exactly with the traffic, so the rule is
     being matched in hardware and is not merely being shadowed by the implicit permit.

